-- allium: 3
--
-- One node's participation in one database's log processing.
--
-- A participant runs ONE loop for as long as it exists. That loop reads the database's
-- replica log and applies what it reads, always. While the participant LEADS, the same
-- loop additionally reads the database's source log, resolves client transactions and
-- appends them to the replica log -- leading is that loop with a writing side present.
--
-- Everything continuous is the participant's, for the participant's whole life: how far the
-- log has been read, the highest term seen there, how far the source log's contents have
-- been applied, and any block still waiting for its upload. Each is a fact about the log,
-- whose lifetime is the database's, so a role change transfers none of them
-- (NothingCrossesARoleChange).
--
-- Leading reaches the apply path at exactly two points. The loop asks whether the writing
-- side is already holding this record (LeadershipAnswersForWhatItWrote), and it asks the
-- writing side to take a block's cut (LeadershipTakesTheCut). With no writing side present
-- both questions have an ordinary answer.
--
-- Who leads is decided OUTSIDE this module, by a transport that watches which nodes are
-- alive and hands the role out. Sections 5 and 6 are that boundary. Nothing here chooses
-- its own role: a participant learns that it has been offered one, and adjudicates the
-- claim it then writes.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- External Entities
------------------------------------------------------------

external entity NodeIdentity {
    -- The node hosting a participant. What distinguishes one participant in a database
    -- from another, which is the situation this module is about.
    name: String
}

external entity Database {
    -- The database whose log this node reads, and may write to. See: db.allium.
    --   Its lifecycle governs the participant: tearing the database down tears the role
    --   and any in-flight claim down with it, so there is no resignation on shutdown.
    name: String
    replica_log: ReplicaLog
    source_log: SourceLog

    -- Where a participant seeds its read position and its term from at startup.
    boundary: PersistedBoundary
}

external entity ReplicaLog {
    -- The database's replica log: the durable, totally-ordered sequence of records every
    -- participant reads to build local state, and that a writing side writes.
    --   A party with obligations (DurableTotalOrder) rather than a participant in the
    --   lifecycle -- who leads is the transport's decision, and the log neither knows nor
    --   cares.
}

external entity SourceLog {
    -- Where clients submit. Read only by a writing side, so only while leading -- the one
    -- asymmetry between the two logs that matters here: reading the replica log is what a
    -- participant always does, and reading the source log is what leading adds.
}

external entity ReplicaRecord {
    -- One record on the replica log, stamped with the term of the writing side that appended it.
    --   The stamp is what every reader fences on, and the only thing about a record this
    --   module reads. What a record MEANS -- a resolved transaction, a block boundary, a
    --   block's upload, tries added or deleted -- belongs to db.allium; this module names
    --   only the kinds whose handling depends on the role.
    term: Integer
    position: Position
}

external entity PersistedBoundary {
    -- The last block boundary durably recorded for this database.
    --   A participant starting up has no records in hand, so this is where both its read
    --   position and its term come from. It is also the floor under retention: reclaiming
    --   a record above it would leave a starting participant unable to catch up, and would
    --   unmoor the term from the log (RetentionKeepsEverythingAboveTheBoundary).
    position: Position
    term: Integer
}

external entity Leadership {
    -- What leading ADDS to the loop: a reader of the source log that resolves client
    -- transactions and appends them to the replica log, stamping each with its term.
    --
    -- Subordinate to the loop, with three consequences, each stated where it arises: the
    -- loop goes on reading and applying
    -- throughout leadership's life (section 4); a record leadership appended is applied by
    -- the loop on reading it back, as any other record is (ApplyRecord); and leadership
    -- ending is not the loop ending (section 6).
    --
    -- It reaches the apply path through the two hooks and nothing else -- it answers for the
    -- records it is still holding (LeadershipAnswersForWhatItWrote), and it takes the cut at
    -- a boundary it wrote (LeadershipTakesTheCut).

    -- The term it stamps on everything it appends. Chosen by the transport, adjudicated
    -- against the log before it is allowed to append (section 5), and thereafter the fence:
    -- reading back a record above it means newer leadership exists and this is finished
    -- (ALeaderIsNeverBehindTheTermItHasSeen).
    term: Integer

    -- How far it has read the source log.
    --   Ahead of what the loop has applied by exactly the records it has appended and not
    --   yet seen come back, which is what WritingRunsAheadOfApplying is about.
    source_position: Position
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Value Types
------------------------------------------------------------

value Position {
    -- A position in a log. Ordered, and reaching one means having been delivered
    -- everything below it (DeliveryFollowsPositionOrder).
    offset: Integer
}

value Claim {
    -- The marker a participant appends when the transport offers it the role, stamped with
    -- the term it has been offered.
    --
    -- Its own position is the whole of its value. A participant must not act on a role
    -- until it has read the log up to the point the role reached it -- otherwise it would
    -- write to a log it had not finished reading -- and a claim gives it a position to read up
    -- to that the log will certainly deliver. Asking the log for its extent instead does
    -- not work: an extent can count positions a reader is never delivered, and a
    -- participant waiting for one of those waits forever.
    term: Integer
    position: Position
}

value HeldBlock {
    -- A block boundary that has been read while its upload has not.
    --
    -- While one is held, records above it are HELD rather than applied. A block is a
    -- consistent cut of local state and the records above the cut belong to the next block,
    -- so applying them before the cut has been taken would fold them into the wrong one.
    -- Holding them is what makes the cut atomic to a reader that is not the one taking it.
    --
    -- Only a boundary this participant did not author is ever held: the author uploads on
    -- reading its own boundary back, so it has nothing to wait for (UploadOwnBoundary).
    boundary_position: Position
    block_index: Integer
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Contracts
------------------------------------------------------------

contract DurableTotalOrder {
    -- What the replica log must supply for a term to mean anything. Obligations on the log
    -- rather than on whoever elects, so they hold however the role is decided.
    --
    -- append returns the position the record now occupies; read_after delivers the records
    -- above a position, in that order.
    append: (log: ReplicaLog, term: Integer) -> Position
    read_after: (log: ReplicaLog, after: Position) -> Set<ReplicaRecord>

    @invariant AppendReturnsItsOwnPosition
        -- An accepted append tells its author which position the record now occupies.
        --   A claim is a term stamped onto a record and then read back, so without the
        --   position coming back from the append the claimant would need a second handshake
        --   to learn where its own marker landed.

    @invariant OneAcceptedAppendYieldsOneRecord
        -- An accepted append puts exactly one record in the log, a refused one puts none,
        -- and the author is told which happened.
        --   A claim that landed twice, or one whose outcome cannot be established, leaves
        --   the claimant reading up to a position other than the one it stamped -- and
        --   writing while believing it has read everything before its own term.

    @invariant OneOrderSeenByEveryReader
        -- Every reader of a log sees the same records at the same positions.
        --   The fence is nothing but readers agreeing on which term the log has reached.
        --   Two readers disagreeing about the order could disagree about that, and a
        --   superseded writing side would go on being acknowledged.

    @invariant DeliveryFollowsPositionOrder
        -- A reader is delivered records in position order, and reaching a position means
        -- having been delivered everything below it.
        --   Independent of OneOrderSeenByEveryReader, which fixes where each record sits
        --   rather than the order a reader meets them in. A reader silently handed a gap
        --   misses the higher-term record that supersedes its writing side, and goes on being
        --   acknowledged for writes every other reader discards -- so a read that would
        --   skip a position has to fail rather than proceed.

    @invariant AcceptedRecordsAreNeverWithdrawn
        -- Once an append is accepted, its record is never removed, replaced or moved while
        -- it stays above the retention boundary.
        --   Ordinarily a durability property; here a safety one, because a term reaches
        --   another node only as a record. Withdrawing one after that node has read it lets
        --   the two disagree about which term the log has reached, which is the one thing
        --   the fence cannot survive.

    @invariant RetentionKeepsEverythingAboveTheBoundary
        -- Records at or above the persisted boundary's position are retained; reclaiming
        -- those below it is expected.
        --   A term is seeded from the boundary and monotonic from there, so this is both
        --   what lets a starting participant catch up at all and what puts a floor under
        --   the fence. Reclaiming a record that still sits above the boundary would unmoor
        --   the highest term seen from the log it is meant to describe.

    @invariant DurabilityComesFromDeployedConfiguration
        -- Expected of the operator: AcceptedRecordsAreNeverWithdrawn has to hold in the
        -- DEPLOYED configuration, and nothing in here can enforce it.
        --   Settings that let an accepted append be lost -- acknowledging before the record
        --   is held widely enough to survive a failure, or letting a replica that never
        --   received it take over -- withdraw a record the log has already promised. Where
        --   the log can report its own settings they are worth checking as the database
        --   connects: caught at startup this is an error message, caught later it is two
        --   nodes disagreeing about the term.
}

contract OneLoop {
    -- What a participant owes the node hosting it: one loop, for as long as the participant
    -- exists, servicing its replica log always and its source log while it leads.
    read_after: (log: ReplicaLog, after: Position) -> Set<ReplicaRecord>

    @invariant ExactlyOneLoopPerParticipant
        -- A participant has one loop in every state, never none and never two.
        --   Two would each hold their own idea of how far the log had been read and which
        --   term it had reached, and the fence is readers agreeing on the second of those.
        --   None, for however short a window, is a participant that has stopped learning
        --   what its log says while remaining a member of the database.

    @invariant TheLoopOutlivesLeadership
        -- The loop is created when the participant is and ends when the participant does.
        -- Taking leadership creates no loop and losing it ends none.
        --   Which is what makes ReadPositionNeverRewinds and TheTermIsNeverForgotten
        --   structural: a value that is never re-seeded cannot be re-seeded wrongly.
        --   It also puts the end of a term inside the loop's life, so whatever was awaiting
        --   the writing side has to be failed at the moment leadership ends rather than
        --   falling out of the loop's own exit (see the open question).

    @invariant LeadershipReachesApplyingThroughTwoHooks
        -- Leadership reaches the apply path at exactly two points: the loop asks whether
        -- leadership is already holding the record it is applying
        -- (LeadershipAnswersForWhatItWrote), and it asks leadership to take a block's cut
        -- (LeadershipTakesTheCut).
        --   Both are questions asked of something that may be absent, and both have an
        --   ordinary answer when it is. Everything else on the apply path -- the fence, the
        --   read position, the source watermark, holding records and releasing them -- is the
        --   same whether or not the participant leads, and consults nothing optional. So a new
        --   kind of record either wants a hook of its own or wants nothing.

    @invariant LeadershipAnswersForWhatItWrote
        -- The first hook. Applying a record, the loop asks leadership whether it is already
        -- holding that record; if it is, the loop takes both the contents and the account of
        -- what has already been done from there rather than from the record.
        --   Leadership resolved the transaction in order to know its result, attached or
        --   detached in order to produce it, added the tries it wrote, removed the tries it
        --   collected, rolled the index in order to write a block out -- so it holds the
        --   materialised form AND the knowledge that those effects have landed. A hit means
        --   commit what is held and advance the watermarks; a miss means do the whole thing
        --   from the record, which is what every participant does for every record it did not
        --   write.
        --   Safe only because a record and what leadership holds are the same thing by
        --   construction -- it appends in resolution order and reads back in position order,
        --   and OneOrderSeenByEveryReader makes those the same order. A hit whose contents
        --   disagree with the record is therefore a fault rather than a case to handle.

    @invariant LeadershipTakesTheCut
        -- The second hook. Reaching a block boundary, the loop asks leadership to take the
        -- cut; with no leadership, or for a boundary leadership did not write, it holds
        -- records until the upload appears instead.
        --   Where the first hook saves work, this one assigns it: a cut is local state as of
        --   exactly one position, and the participant that wrote the boundary is the one whose
        --   state that is, so it is the only one that can write the cut out. It is also the one
        --   effect the first hook could not have carried, because reaching that state means
        --   having read up to that position, which is what reading the boundary back is.

    @invariant NothingCrossesARoleChange
        -- A role change transfers no state. It starts a writing side or stops one, and that is
        -- all.
        --   Everything continuous belongs to the participant and is untouched: the read
        --   position, the highest term seen, how far the source log has been applied, and any
        --   block being held. What would falsify it is any value a rule in section 5 or 6 has
        --   to copy from one place to another.

    @invariant ReadPositionNeverRewinds
        -- A participant's read position only ever advances, for the whole life of the
        -- participant.
        --   Every rule that sets it sets it to the position of the record just read, and
        --   delivery is in position order (DeliveryFollowsPositionOrder). Nothing re-seeds it,
        --   because nothing re-creates the loop (TheLoopOutlivesLeadership).
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities and Variants
------------------------------------------------------------

entity Participant {
    -- One node's participation in one database's log processing.
    --   Every node has one per database it hosts, and it starts out not leading.
    node: NodeIdentity
    database: Database

    -- not_leading  the loop, with no writing side. No role and no claim.
    -- prepared     the transport has offered the role and the claim has conferred; a term is
    --              held, but nothing has been appended at it yet.
    -- leading      the loop, with a writing side present.
    state: not_leading | prepared | leading

    transitions state {
        not_leading -> prepared  -- offered the role, and the claim conferred (section 5)
        prepared -> not_leading  -- the offer was withdrawn (section 5)
        prepared -> leading      -- the transport confirmed the role (section 5)
        leading -> not_leading   -- demoted, superseded, or the writing side failed (section 6)
        -- No terminal state: a participant cycles indefinitely.
    }

    -- How far this participant has read and applied its replica log.
    --   Belongs to the participant rather than to a role, because it is a fact about the
    --   log, whose lifetime is the database's. Monotonic for the participant's whole life
    --   (ReadPositionNeverRewinds).
    read_position: Position

    -- The highest term any record it has read carried.
    --   Seeded from the persisted boundary and only ever raised (TheTermIsNeverForgotten).
    --   Held here for the same reason as the read position, and it is the same reader that
    --   teaches it both: a term learned before this node led is still known while it leads.
    highest_term_seen: Integer

    -- How far the source log's contents have been applied here, as the records this
    -- participant has applied report it.
    --   Leadership resumes the source log from exactly this, so it is the third thing a role
    --   change would otherwise have had to hand over. It is also what a client waiting for
    --   its transaction to become visible is waiting on.
    source_position: Position

    -- Set between reading a block boundary someone else authored and reading that block's
    -- upload.
    --   Genuinely optional rather than state-dependent: a participant can hold a block in
    --   every state, and one held across a role change is exactly the case two role-shaped
    --   loops would have to hand over and this one does not.
    held_block: HeldBlock?

    claim: Claim when state = prepared
    leadership: Leadership when state = leading

    -- Derived: this participant has a writing side.
    is_leading: state = leading

    -- Derived: records are being held rather than applied.
    is_holding: held_block != null
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules
--
-- 4. Reading, which is what a participant always does
--
-- One trigger -- a record was read -- and the handling of it. Leadership appears in this
-- section only through its two hooks: it answers for a record it is still holding, and it
-- takes the cut at a boundary it wrote.
------------------------------------------------------------

rule ApplyRecord {
    -- The ordinary case, and the one that is the same in every state.
    when: RecordRead(participant, record)

    requires: record.term >= participant.highest_term_seen
    requires: participant.held_block = null

    ensures:
        -- The fence is decided against the highest term seen STRICTLY BEFORE this record,
        -- and then raised. One act and not two: raising first would leave nothing to
        -- compare against, and a reader comparing against a term that already included
        -- this record's own would admit everything.
        if record.term > participant.highest_term_seen:
            participant.highest_term_seen = record.term

        -- The first hook, asked here and nowhere else. If leadership is holding this record
        -- -- because it is the record leadership just appended -- the contents and the
        -- account of what has already been done come from there, and applying it is
        -- committing what is held and advancing the watermarks. Otherwise the record itself
        -- is the whole of the input.
        --   Not a branch on the state: it is one question with an ordinary answer when there
        --   is nothing to ask (LeadershipAnswersForWhatItWrote), which is why a
        --   new kind of record needs no decision here.
        RecordApplied(participant: participant, record: record)
        participant.read_position = record.position

    @guidance
        -- What "applied" covers -- committing a transaction to the live index, refreshing a
        -- catalogue, advancing the source watermark -- is db.allium's and
        -- live-index.allium's subject rather than this module's. What matters here is that
        -- it happens once per record, in position order, by one loop.
}

rule DiscardFencedRecord {
    -- A record below the term already seen was appended by leadership since superseded, so it
    -- is discarded -- and the position still advances.
    when: RecordRead(participant, record)

    requires: record.term < participant.highest_term_seen

    ensures:
        -- Discarding suppresses APPLICATION, not CONSUMPTION. A participant that stopped
        -- advancing over discarded records would never reach a claim sitting above one, and
        -- would wait on it forever.
        participant.read_position = record.position
}

rule UploadOwnBoundary {
    -- The second hook: a boundary this participant's own leadership wrote is one it takes the
    -- cut for, on reading it back.
    --   LeadershipTakesTheCut, as a rule: the author is the node whose local state IS the
    --   cut, so it is the only node that can write the cut out. Nothing is held, because
    --   there is nothing to wait for.
    when: BlockBoundaryRead(participant, record, block_index)

    requires: participant.is_leading
    -- Own means at our own term. A boundary above it belongs to newer leadership and
    -- supersedes us (ResignOnSupersession); one below it is fenced. So the three cases a
    -- boundary can fall into are decided by the same stamp everything else is.
    requires: record.term = participant.leadership.term
    requires: participant.held_block = null

    ensures:
        BlockUploadedFrom(participant: participant, block_index: block_index)
        participant.read_position = record.position
}

rule HoldForeignBoundary {
    -- A boundary someone else authored is a cut this participant waits for.
    when: BlockBoundaryRead(participant, record, block_index)

    requires: participant.held_block = null
    -- Anything that is not our own boundary, which is every boundary at all unless we are
    -- writing at its term. Leadership reading a boundary ABOVE its term resigns first and the
    -- loop then holds this one by the ordinary path, having lost the hook that would have
    -- taken the cut.
    requires: participant.state = leading implies record.term != participant.leadership.term

    ensures:
        participant.held_block = HeldBlock.created(
            boundary_position: record.position,
            block_index: block_index
        )
        participant.read_position = record.position
}

rule HoldRecordDuringBlock {
    -- Everything read while a block is held is kept, in order, to be applied when it is
    -- released.
    when: RecordRead(participant, record)

    requires: participant.held_block != null

    ensures:
        RecordHeld(participant: participant, record: record)
        participant.read_position = record.position
}

rule ReleaseHeldBlock {
    -- The cut has been taken; apply what was held, in the order it was read.
    when: BlockUploadRead(participant, record, block_index)

    requires: participant.held_block != null

    ensures:
        participant.held_block = null
        HeldRecordsApplied(participant: participant)
        participant.read_position = record.position
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- 5. Taking the role
--
-- The transport offers, the log adjudicates, the transport confirms. The participant's
-- reading is untouched throughout.
------------------------------------------------------------

rule AcceptOfferAndClaim {
    -- The transport has decided this participant should lead, and names the term.
    when: TransportOfferedRole(transport, participant, term)

    requires: participant.state = not_leading

    ensures:
        -- Append a marker stamped with the offered term, and take its position as
        -- something to read up to. Nothing is stopped and nothing is built: this is one
        -- append and a position to wait for.
        let position = ClaimAppended(participant: participant, term: term)

        -- Read up to the claim. The only unbounded step in taking the role, and the reason
        -- the claim exists: a participant must have read everything the log said before the
        -- role reached it, or it would write to a log it had not finished reading.
        ReadUpTo(participant: participant, target: position)

        -- Adjudicate. By here the claimed term is expected to BE the highest seen -- the
        -- claim itself carried it -- so a strictly higher one belongs to another writing side,
        -- and everything this one appended would be discarded by every reader. Refusing
        -- costs liveness only and never safety, so it is sound to decide from whatever has
        -- been read.
        ClaimAdjudicated(participant: participant, term: term)

        participant.claim = Claim.created(term: term, position: position)
        participant.state = prepared
}

rule DeclineFencedOffer {
    -- The log had already moved past the offered term, so the role is declined and the
    -- transport is told.
    when: ClaimRefused(participant, term)

    requires: participant.state = not_leading

    ensures:
        -- There is nothing to undo. A participant that had stopped reading in order to
        -- claim would have to start again here; this one never stopped, and the claim it
        -- appended is inert -- a marker at a term nobody writes at moves no reader's fence
        -- beyond where the term already was.
        RoleDeclined(participant: participant, term: term)
}

rule TakeLeadership {
    -- The transport confirms, and the writing side starts.
    when: TransportConfirmedRole(transport, participant)

    requires: participant.state = prepared
    requires: participant.held_block = null

    ensures:
        -- Leadership resumes the source log from what this participant has APPLIED, not from
        -- what its predecessor had resolved. Applied is the only safe answer: a position
        -- applied here is one whose effects are in local state, and resuming above it would
        -- skip source records whose transactions previous leadership resolved and whose work
        -- never came back.
        participant.leadership = Leadership.created(
            term: participant.claim.term,
            source_position: participant.source_position
        )
        participant.claim = null
        participant.state = leading
}

rule FinishWhatWasLeftBehind {
    -- A boundary PREVIOUS leadership wrote and never uploaded is finished by the next, before
    -- it appends anything of its own.
    --   Without this the block would never be uploaded and every participant would hold
    --   records above it indefinitely. The predecessor's local state is gone, but the
    --   boundary names the cut and this participant has read every record below it -- so
    --   its own state at that cut is the same state. That is the whole reason a loop which
    --   never restarts can finish another's work.
    --
    --   Nothing special happens to the records being held. This participant appends the
    --   upload like any other, reads it back like any other, and releases them by the
    --   ordinary path (ReleaseHeldBlock) -- so a role change landing mid-block needs no
    --   third way of applying a record.
    when: TransportConfirmedRole(transport, participant)

    requires: participant.state = prepared
    requires: participant.held_block != null

    ensures:
        BlockUploadedFrom(participant: participant, block_index: participant.held_block.block_index)
        participant.held_block = null
        participant.leadership = Leadership.created(
            term: participant.claim.term,
            source_position: participant.source_position
        )
        participant.claim = null
        participant.state = leading
}

rule WithdrawOffer {
    -- The transport took the offer back before confirming it.
    when: TransportWithdrewOffer(transport, participant)

    requires: participant.state = prepared

    ensures:
        -- Nothing to recover, and nothing was stopped in the first place.
        participant.claim = null
        participant.state = not_leading
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- 6. Leaving the role
--
-- Three ways leadership ends, and one thing they share: the loop carries on reading. That is
-- what NothingCrossesARoleChange means in practice -- there is no position to hand over,
-- because the thing that would have received it was never gone.
------------------------------------------------------------

rule Demote {
    -- The transport revoked the role while the node stays up.
    --   Not on shutdown, where tearing the database down tears the loop down with it.
    when: TransportRevokedRole(transport, participant)

    requires: participant.state = leading

    ensures:
        LeadershipStopped(participant: participant)
        participant.leadership = null
        participant.state = not_leading
}

rule ResignOnSupersession {
    -- A record above this leadership's term means newer leadership exists.
    when: RecordRead(participant, record)

    requires: participant.state = leading
    requires: record.term > participant.leadership.term

    ensures:
        -- Leadership ends and the loop goes on reading -- including applying this very record,
        -- which is the new leadership's and which ApplyRecord handles as it would any other.
        -- Being superseded is therefore not an interruption in reading at all. It is the
        -- moment the first hook stops having an answer.
        LeadershipStopped(participant: participant)
        participant.leadership = null
        participant.state = not_leading
}

rule StopOnLeadershipFailure {
    -- Leadership itself failed: it could not read the source log, resolve a transaction, or
    -- append what it had resolved.
    when: LeadershipFailed(node, participant)

    requires: participant.state = leading

    ensures:
        LeadershipStopped(participant: participant)
        participant.leadership = null
        participant.state = not_leading

    @guidance
        -- What failed leadership should do to the DATABASE is deliberately not settled here --
        -- see the open question. This rule says only what the structure makes true: leadership
        -- ending is not the loop ending, so whatever is decided about the database is a
        -- decision rather than a consequence of how the pieces are arranged.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Invariants
------------------------------------------------------------

invariant ARoleIsBackedByWhatItNeeds {
    -- Whatever state the participant is in, it holds the thing that state is about.
    for p in Participants:
        (p.state = prepared implies p.claim != null)
        and (p.state = leading implies p.leadership != null)
}

invariant TheTermIsNeverForgotten {
    -- The highest term seen is at or above the persisted boundary's, always.
    --   A participant seeds it from the boundary once and only ever raises it. The failure
    --   this rules out is specific: a term held per role is re-seeded from the boundary at
    --   every role change, forgetting every term written since the last block was flushed
    --   -- so the same term could be admitted twice, once either side of a demote, and only
    --   ever under contention.
    for p in Participants:
        p.highest_term_seen >= p.database.boundary.term
}

invariant ALeaderIsNeverBehindTheTermItHasSeen {
    -- A leading participant's term is at or above the highest term it has read.
    --   This is the fence, stated as a property of a state rather than of a moment: the
    --   instant it would be violated -- a record above leadership's term is read -- is the
    --   instant leadership ends (ResignOnSupersession). Two participants writing to one log
    --   at once is not prevented; what is prevented is both being acknowledged, because the
    --   older one reads the younger one's records and stops.
    for p in Participants:
        p.state = leading implies p.leadership.term >= p.highest_term_seen
}

invariant WritingRunsAheadOfApplying {
    -- Leadership's source position is at or above what the participant has applied.
    --   The gap is the records it has appended and not yet read back. It closes as they come
    --   back and reopens as it appends more, so it is not an inconsistency but the reason a
    --   writing side is worth having at all: resolving the next transaction does not wait on
    --   the last one's round trip.
    for p in Participants:
        p.state = leading implies p.leadership.source_position.offset >= p.source_position.offset
}

invariant AHeldBlockSitsBehindTheReader {
    -- A block is held only from a boundary already read.
    for p in Participants:
        p.is_holding implies p.held_block.boundary_position.offset <= p.read_position.offset
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Actor Declarations
------------------------------------------------------------

actor Transport {
    -- Whatever decides which node leads which database. It watches liveness, hands the role
    -- out, takes it back, and supplies the term.
    --   Modelled as an external actor because nothing in this module chooses its own role.
    --   That the decision is external is the assumption most likely to be revisited: a
    --   participant which adjudicates its own claim against the log is already doing the
    --   hard half, and the transport supplies only liveness and a counter.
    identified_by: Database where name != ""
}

actor Node {
    -- The node hosting the participant, and the party its reading and writing report to.
    identified_by: NodeIdentity where name != ""
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Surfaces
------------------------------------------------------------

surface RoleHandout {
    -- The transport's boundary: offer, confirm, withdraw, revoke.
    --   Four operations and no state coming back. What the transport learns is whether an
    --   offer was taken up, and it learns that from the participant reaching the confirmed
    --   role or declining. It is not told the participant's position, its term, or anything
    --   else about the log, because none of that is its business.
    facing transport: Transport

    context participant: Participant

    exposes:
        participant.state

    provides:
        TransportOfferedRole(transport, participant, term) when participant.state = not_leading
        TransportConfirmedRole(transport, participant) when participant.state = prepared
        TransportWithdrewOffer(transport, participant) when participant.state = prepared
        TransportRevokedRole(transport, participant) when participant.state = leading

    @guarantee ARoleChangeIsCheap
        -- Taking up an offer costs one append and reading up to it. Confirming it starts a
        -- writing side; revoking it stops one.
        --   None of the four stops the loop, so the transport cannot make a node stop learning
        --   what its log says by changing its mind about who leads.

    @guarantee LeadershipEndingDisturbsNoOtherDatabase
        -- Leadership ending -- demoted, superseded, or failed -- affects its own database and no
        -- other, whatever the transport shares between them.
        --   A transport that multiplexes several databases over one connection has a failure
        --   path that does not respect the boundary: leadership can end from the loop's own
        --   reading while a request from that
        --   shared connection is still outstanding, and the failure then surfaces on the
        --   connection rather than on the participant. Ending a term is routine and must not be
        --   able to cost anybody else their database, so the conversion of that failure is part
        --   of this boundary rather than an implementation detail behind it.

    @guarantee OnlyTheTermComesFromOutside
        -- The transport supplies the term and nothing else; whether anything may be appended
        -- at that term is decided against the log.
        --   So a transport whose counter has gone backwards cannot install leadership below the
        --   term the log has reached: the offer is declined instead
        --   (DeclineFencedOffer). This is the boundary's one substantive check, and the
        --   reason the transport is trusted with liveness but not with safety.
}

surface ReadingAndWriting {
    -- The participant's own reading and writing, reported to the node hosting them.
    --   Every rule in section 4 keys off a read, and the failure rule in section 6 off
    --   leadership ending. Nothing else drives this module.
    facing node: Node

    context participant: Participant

    exposes:
        participant.read_position
        participant.highest_term_seen
        participant.source_position

    provides:
        RecordRead(participant, record)
        BlockBoundaryRead(participant, record, block_index)
        BlockUploadRead(participant, record, block_index)
        ClaimRefused(participant, term)
        LeadershipFailed(node, participant) when participant.state = leading

    contracts:
        demands DurableTotalOrder
        fulfils OneLoop

    @guarantee ReadingNeedsNothingButAnOrderedLog
        -- Read forward in order; append, and be told where the append landed. That is the
        -- whole of what this module asks of a log.
        --   No membership protocol, no coordination service, and nothing a log must know
        --   about its own extent. So the reading half of this module is portable to any
        --   durable ordered log, and only the transport's half is not.

    @guarantee ObservabilityAttachesToTheParticipant
        -- What is worth watching is one participant's position, term and source watermark,
        -- each continuous for the participant's life.
        --   A measurement attached to a role restarts when the role changes, so a gap in it
        --   cannot be told apart from a node that has stopped reading. Attached to the
        --   participant, the same numbers answer both questions.
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Open Questions
------------------------------------------------------------

open question "What should a failed writing side do to its database? StopOnLeadershipFailure returns the participant to not leading, which is the structural answer and deliberately not the whole one. Three positions are defensible. (a) Follow on: the participant can still read, so a database that goes on serving queries while refusing writes is a real and useful state, and the transport will hand the role elsewhere. (b) Fail the database: whatever broke the writing side -- a source log it cannot read, a transaction it cannot resolve -- may equally break the next writing side here, and quietly returning to not leading turns a hard failure into a node that looks healthy while never leading again. (c) Fail the database only for faults that are the node's rather than the log's. The reason to leave it open is that (a) falls out of the arrangement while (b) is probably what an operator wants, and choosing between them is a decision about failure policy rather than about structure -- so it wants making deliberately, and separately from the change that made it a choice at all."
